# THE HARD CONTEST POINTS, and the pieces they are built from.
#
# Every point is a program on which plain call-by-name weak
# head reduction is slow or runs forever, and whose answer is still
# well defined: a head (a Scott boolean or natural of the prelude), or no
# head at all. As in `contest_clauses.pl`, a coinductive claim is written
# as a search for a refutation, so its greatest-fixed-point answer "the
# claim holds" is the absence of a head, read `failed` by a tabled
# evaluation that recognises the recursion coming back.
#
# Pieces of `contest_clauses.pl` (numerals, labelled lists, the subtype
# check) are reached by module-qualified calls into `contest`; the prelude
# through `use_module`. EVERY BINDER IS A DISTINCT PROLOG VARIABLE within
# one clause, and a piece used twice is called twice.
#
# Each point's comment gives its intended answer and why, what makes it
# hard for plain weak head reduction, and which mechanism it is aimed
# at. The table of intended answers and limits is
# `contest_hard_answers.pl`.
#
# TARGET ALGORITHMS, the specific known algorithms the problem targets, each
# with its prior work:
# - H1, H2, H8, H10, H11, H19, H27: minimising the automaton of a rational
#   value whose spelling grows or repeats late: R. Paige, R. E. Tarjan,
#   Three Partition Refinement Algorithms, SIAM J. Computing 1987,
#   doi:10.1137/0216062; F. Bonchi, D. Pous, Checking NFA Equivalence with
#   Bisimulations up to Congruence, POPL 2013, doi:10.1145/2429069.2429124.
# - H3, H6, H9, H13, H17: sharing under call by need: J. Launchbury, A
#   Natural Semantics for Lazy Evaluation, POPL 1993,
#   doi:10.1145/158511.158618; Z. M. Ariola, M. Felleisen, J. Maraist, M.
#   Odersky, P. Wadler, A Call-by-Need Lambda Calculus, POPL 1995,
#   doi:10.1145/199448.199507.
# - H4: checking EG good on the graph of states: E. M. Clarke, E. A.
#   Emerson, A. P. Sistla, Automatic Verification of Finite-State Concurrent
#   Systems Using Temporal Logic Specifications, TOPLAS 1986,
#   doi:10.1145/5397.5399.
# - H5, H13, H14, H17: driving with generalisation, which folds a path whose
#   arguments are rebuilt: V. F. Turchin, The Concept of a Supercompiler,
#   TOPLAS 1986, doi:10.1145/5956.5957.
# - H7: tabled evaluation: W. Chen, D. S. Warren, Tabled Evaluation with
#   Delaying for General Logic Programs, J. ACM 1996,
#   doi:10.1145/227595.227597.
# - H9: circular programs: R. S. Bird, Using Circular Programs to Eliminate
#   Multiple Traversals of Data, Acta Informatica 1984,
#   doi:10.1007/BF00264249.
# - H12: productivity of stream definitions: J. Endrullis, C. Grabmayer, D.
#   Hendriks, A. Isihara, J. W. Klop, Productivity of Stream Definitions,
#   TCS 2010, doi:10.1016/j.tcs.2009.10.014.
# - H16, H23, H26: a JIT of the interpreter's hot loops: C. F. Bolz, A.
#   Cuni, M. Fijalkowski, A. Rigo, Tracing the Meta-Level: PyPy's Tracing
#   JIT Compiler, ICOOOLPS 2009, doi:10.1145/1565824.1565827.
# - H18: dynamic programming by memoisation: D. Michie, "Memo" Functions and
#   Machine Learning, Nature 1968, doi:10.1038/218019a0.
# - H20: regular expressions by derivatives, compared by bisimulation: J. A.
#   Brzozowski, Derivatives of Regular Expressions, J. ACM 1964,
#   doi:10.1145/321239.321249; F. Bonchi, D. Pous, Checking NFA Equivalence
#   with Bisimulations up to Congruence, POPL 2013,
#   doi:10.1145/2429069.2429124.
# - H22, H24, H28: coinductive certificates confirmed by the equations: D.
#   Kozen, A. Silva, Practical Coinduction, Mathematical Structures in
#   Computer Science 2016, doi:10.1017/S0960129515000493.
# - H15: coinductive equality of cyclic trees, read as the program's
#   sequential conjunction computes it: D. Kozen, A. Silva, Practical
#   Coinduction, Mathematical Structures in Computer Science 2016,
#   doi:10.1017/S0960129515000493.
# - H29, H30: closing an assumption only on the nearest ancestor judgment,
#   as coinductive logic programming does: L. Simon, A. Mallya, A. Bansal,
#   G. Gupta, Coinductive Logic Programming, ICLP 2006,
#   doi:10.1007/11799573_25.

